<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- SpotBugs project decisions: a rule, or a rule in part of the code, that the team has decided not
     to enforce. One Match per decision, with a comment giving the reason, for example:

       <!- - JPA entities share associations by design (reason ...) - ->
       <Match><Package name="~team\.projectpulse\..*"/><Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/></Match>

     Unlike the baseline, this file may grow, but every entry is a reviewed decision. -->
<FindBugsFilter>
    <!-- D1 (2026-11-13): EI_EXPOSE_REP and EI_EXPOSE_REP2 are off project-wide. On 2026-11-01 the
         219 findings fell into three groups, and in none of them is a defensive copy correct:
         - 158 are JPA entities (Course.getSections(), UseCase.getLock(), ...). A getter must hand back
           the managed association: Hibernate tracks changes to that exact collection, orphanRemoval
           deletes what is removed from it, and services such as UseCaseService.updateUseCase mutate it
           in place. A copy would silently drop those writes.
         - 27 are Spring beans injected through a constructor (a controller holding its service). The
           bean is a shared singleton by design; sharing it is the point of injection.
         - 34 are DTO records and response classes holding Lists. Converters build them and Jackson
           serializes them within one request; nothing keeps a reference to mutate later.
         The remaining handful are the same shape: report holders built per request
         (PeerEvaluationAverage, WeeklyPeerEvaluationReport, ReminderService.ReminderResult), the
         security principal wrapping its user entity (MyUserPrincipal), and DocumentTemplate, whose
         sections are loaded once at startup and only read.
         Check: grep the 2026-11-01 spotbugs-baseline.xml (git show c1d9be9:backend/config/spotbugs-baseline.xml)
         for EI_EXPOSE and look at each Class. -->
    <Match>
        <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP,EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
    </Match>
</FindBugsFilter>
